# Implement read-only mailbox workflows

## Goal

Ship useful read access before any mailbox mutation is enabled.

## Required work

- Implement mailbox and label listing.
- Implement paginated message and thread search.
- Implement message, header, and attachment retrieval.
- Implement conversation retrieval with explicit mapping between Proton
  conversations and provider-neutral threads.
- Bound body and attachment sizes and sanitize filenames.
- Preserve stable identifiers across pagination and reconnects.

## Busy controls must not receive programmatic read-only clicks

- Red-first synthetic Node DOM fixtures showed that Next and sort controls
  inside an `aria-busy="true"` ancestor still received programmatic clicks
  even though that accessibility state marks content as transitional. Audited
  sort-menu state and open/close checks, and Next click, now refuse busy
  ancestry independently of layout, opacity, pointer events or hit testing.
  The test exercises only synthetic elements with no provider interaction.

## Transitional ARIA-busy Mail regions are not settled snapshots

- Red-first synthetic DOM fixtures showed that a loaded root or row with
  `aria-busy="true"` could still project message IDs or read title attributes
  when the previous rows remained mounted during an update. The Mail list and
  title projections now reject busy loaded roots and row subtrees before
  releasing private metadata; explicitly loading-only state remains
  content-free. No live account or external provider response was used.

## Legacy CSS clipping must not prove visible read authority

- Red-first synthetic Node tests exposed the legacy positioned-element
  `clip: rect(0px,0px,0px,0px)` blind spot: Mail rows, private title nodes,
  pagination controls and sort toggles retained positive client rectangles,
  yet their painted pixels were fully clipped. The bounded ancestor gates now
  reject non-default legacy `clip` as well as the modern `clip-path`; ordinary
  un-clipped fixtures remain valid. No live provider browser was used.

## Occluded visible list and title evidence

- Red-first synthetic browser fixtures exposed a mismatch between layout-visible
  nodes and user-visible Mail evidence: an unrelated overlay could cover a
  loaded row, current page, explicit-empty marker, subject or sender while the
  adapter still projected IDs or read private title attributes. Bounded DOM
  projections now require a real same-element or descendant viewport hit on
  each evidence node; off-viewport and covered titles are refused before
  reading content attributes. Existing ordinary visible fixtures remain
  accepted and no live account or provider request was involved.

## Enabled Next readiness must agree with viewport hit testing

- A red-first synthetic list DOM reported enabled Next while an overlay
  intercepted its visual midpoint. The subsequent audited click now refuses
  such controls, so readiness must not claim they are actionable. Content-free
  list evidence now requires the same finite viewport and descendant hit for
  enabled Next, without losing an actually disabled Next behind an overlay.
  The fixture also rejects an off-viewport control without a hit target.

## Mask-hidden Mail content and controls

- Red-first synthetic Node fixtures proved that a CSS `mask-image` rendering
  fully transparent pixels still left ordinary DOM rectangles and CSS opacity
  intact. The loaded Mail list, private row titles, Next pagination, and sort
  menu all treated those hidden elements as visible. Their bounded ancestor
  tests now reject non-default `mask-image` (including the WebKit alias)
  before release or navigation; ordinary unmasked fixtures still pass.
  No provider account or live browser was involved.

## Hit-test programmatic read-only navigation controls

- Red-first Node DOM fixtures showed that Next and sort menu controls
  remained programmatically clickable beneath an unrelated overlay; bounding
  rectangles, visibility, and pointer styles on the controls all passed.
  The audited sort and pagination controls now require a finite viewport
  midpoint and a same-element or descendant `elementFromPoint` hit before
  trusting the menu state or issuing `.click()`. Covered controls cannot
  activate navigation or a sort menu even when their own geometry is intact.
  The fixture uses only synthetic page state and performs no provider I/O.

## Hidden CSS-composited mailbox controls and content

- Red-first executable Node browser fixtures showed that a parent with
  `clip-path: inset(100%)` could hide Mail rows, pagination controls and sort
  options while every child retained nonempty, positive-area layout boxes.
  A parent with `filter: opacity(0)` similarly hid private row titles and
  controls without changing computed `opacity` or the rectangles. The audited
  bounded ancestor gates now refuse non-default clip paths and CSS filters
  before either exposing Mail row metadata or triggering read-only clicks.
  Synthetic tests retain ordinary, unfiltered controls; no live account or
  browser was involved.

## Collapsed content is not a visible mailbox snapshot

- Red-first executable synthetic browser fixtures exposed nonempty but
  zero-area client rectangles on displayed list rows, current pagination
  controls, empty placeholders, and message subject/sender nodes. Previously
  these elements could establish list readiness or release title content.
  Shared positive-area checks now reject this geometry before exposing list
  evidence or reading sensitive titles. Ordinary visible fixtures remain valid;
  no live provider, account, or mailbox data was involved.

## Collapsed Next controls are not list readiness evidence

- A red-first executable synthetic list proved that a zero-width/height Next
  button with a nonempty DOMRectList still reported enabled, contradicting the
  action's positive-area preflight. The content-free list projection now rejects
  this geometry before claiming a settled actionable page; an ordinarily
  disabled, positive-area Next control remains observable. No browser account
  interaction was involved.

## Zero-area control safety

- Red-first executable synthetic DOM tests proved that zero-width/height
  client rectangles still passed the existing nonempty geometry gate for
  programmatic Next and sort-menu clicks. Nonempty DOMRects do not necessarily
  mean a painted, user-actionable control (for example, scale-zero transforms).
  The audited actions now require at least one positive-area client rectangle
  for each needed control, including the sort options proving menu state.
  No live provider browser, account or mailbox was used.

## Enabled Next evidence and ancestor pointer blocking

- The content-free list used to report enabled Next when the button overrode
  a parent `pointer-events:none` with `auto`; its eventual click was correctly
  refused by the audited script, but the readiness claim was contradictory.
  Red-first executable DOM coverage now verifies that the list rejects that
  parent override, while a disabled Next can remain visible and report disabled.
  The bounded list pointer ancestry check matches the click preflight and
  does not add any browser or provider I/O.

## Pre-CDP visible-list and row projection gates

- Red-first executable synthetic DOM tests demonstrated that loaded list IDs,
  visible subject and sender projections could still be returned from inside
  a `content-visibility:hidden` ancestor. Both read-only DOM snapshots now
  reject this state at each bounded ancestor before serializing any content.
  The same tests cover semicolon matrix-style provider IDs, which the Rust
  route guard already rejected, but pre-CDP `invalidId` expressions had still
  permitted. Both JavaScript predicates now match the shared Rust guard.

## Skip-rendering subtree gate

- Additional red-first executable DOM fixtures exposed a separate hidden
  control path: `content-visibility:hidden` on a parent still allowed a
  descendant's programmatic Next-page or sort-menu click even though the
  subtree is not rendered. The audited click-ancestry checks now reject that
  CSS state before any activation, and the existing ordinary enabled-control
  fixtures remain compatible. No provider account, real browser, or write
  action was involved.

## Programmatic click ancestry checks

- Red-first executable synthetic DOM fixtures exposed a mismatch between
  user-actionable controls and `.click()` in pagination and sort-menu actions:
  CSS `pointer-events:none` on a parent did not prevent the read adapter from
  activating an otherwise visible child. All three audited click scripts now
  require the entire bounded ancestor chain to have pointer events enabled,
  in addition to existing opacity, visibility, native disabled, ARIA, form and
  origin checks. Next-page and sort open/close JS fixture matrices exercise
  this without launching a real browser or contacting Proton.

## Exact provider path-component identity

- Red-first synthetic message-list and exact-detail GET fixtures showed that
  semicolon-delimited matrix parameters could be retained as opaque Mail IDs,
  including labels and attachment parent references. Since HTTP routers can
  reinterpret a semicolon as path-segment metadata, the shared read-ID guard
  now rejects it before accepting any bound provider identifier. The existing
  Base64-style `+`/`=` IDs and printable Unicode remain valid. No account or
  provider network access was involved.

## Current evidence

- Private CDP request bookkeeping no longer derives unsanitized `Debug`
  for message-list continuation anchors, message/conversation/attachment
  identifiers, or per-capture URL fingerprints. Explicit implementations
  retain only structural state and redact private fields before any accidental
  diagnostic formatting. Red-first synthetic private-record tests prove an
  event ID and a message anchor were previously emitted in their entirety.

- Strict provider/browser JSON objects now reject repeated keys as soon as
  the repeated key is decoded, before parsing its value. A duplicate cannot
  induce parsing of a large or malformed nested value; the rejected key and
  previously accepted private fields remain scrub-on-drop. Synthetic nested
  and malformed-value precedence tests prove the early refusal.

- Mailbox list readiness/evidence now validates and deduplicates borrowed row
  IDs before checking skeleton state and pagination. It allocates copies only
  once the entire observation is accepted into `MailboxListEvidence`, whose
  Drop scrubs every ID. Invalid late selector metadata cannot leave ordinary
  unprotected row-ID allocations on an error path.

- Visible mailbox row projection now borrows ID, subject, and address text
  until all per-row checks, including the unread bit, pass. Accepted rows
  allocate only inside `VisibleMailboxRow`, whose explicit Drop scrubs text.
  Malformed late fields no longer discard ordinary temporary private Strings;
  stable page and repeat-observation checks remain unchanged.

- Message-list Network continuation `AnchorID` now stays borrowed from
  guarded event JSON throughout query-shape parsing, context hashing,
  capacity checks, and response matching. Only accepted tracked requests
  allocate an owned `Zeroizing<String>`; malformed or rejected continuation
  selectors no longer drop ordinary temporary private query strings.

- Catalog item projection now borrows provider IDs and names from the
  scrub-on-drop strict JSON tree through identifier, display text, kind,
  order, and parent validation. Only validated entries receive private
  String copies inside scrub-on-drop catalog items. Late rejection no longer
  drops unguarded temporary IDs and names; numeric and string parent
  semantics and combined folder-graph checks remain unchanged.

- Message-detail projection now borrows bounded IDs, subject, recipient
  display/address fields, label IDs, and nested attachment text directly from
  the strict scrub-on-drop provider JSON until validation succeeds. Only
  accepted metadata and descriptors allocate their scrub-on-drop owned copies;
  malformed late recipients, duplicate labels, bad parent IDs, and invalid
  MIME types no longer release ordinary unprotected temporary Strings.

- Complete conversation membership and attachment metadata projectors now
  validate provider IDs, parent IDs, filename and media text by borrowing
  fields from guarded strict JSON. They only allocate identifier copies into
  the final scrub-on-drop metadata after their respective checks succeed,
  including duplicate, message-parent, filename, and MIME bounds. Rejected
  responses no longer drop unguarded intermediate private Strings.

- Passive message, conversation, and attachment detail CDP envelope projectors
  now reuse their exact body validators with already validated entity selectors.
  They no longer allocate and drop reconstructed full GET URLs containing
  private entity IDs. Standalone exact-URL parsers retain endpoint validation;
  synthetic parity tests cover all three normalized projections and preserve
  their existing content-minimization and ID-binding rules.

- Event/catalog cursor decoding now uses the bounded Base64 slice API on an
  already zeroizing output buffer, including partially written prefixes on
  malformed-token errors. Successful decoding erases unused capacity before
  truncation so drop scrubs the exact retained bytes. Synthetic guarded-type,
  valid-token, and invalid-suffix tests protect both codec implementations;
  existing authenticated cursor scope and tamper tests remain unchanged.

- Encrypted event/catalog cursor encoders now assemble final bearer-token
  text directly inside one pre-sized zeroizing String, not through unguarded
  temporary Base64 and formatted Strings. A checked encoded-length preflight
  rejects oversized tokens before allocation; successful tokens transfer their
  existing allocation to the caller without cloning. Synthetic private type,
  framing, and oversized-token tests preserve cursor compatibility. Public
  caller-owned token strings remain outside the codec's drop-time custody.

- Event and catalog cursor JSON serialization now uses a bounded-growth
  zeroizing writer that erases earlier plaintext allocations before replacing
  capacity. Both codecs reserve the AES-GCM tag's 16 bytes before encrypting
  in place, preventing a late private-buffer reallocation. Synthetic writer
  growth, overflow refusal, and existing authenticated round trips preserve
  exact cursor behavior without provider access.

- Authenticated cursor encoding now encrypts the existing zeroizing JSON
  buffer in place for both event and catalog continuation tokens. The AEAD
  convenience API could first allocate an ordinary plaintext copy and release
  it on encryption errors. Synthetic type-bound encrypted round trips verify
  that ciphertext is produced from guarded storage without changing tokens.

- Authenticated event and catalog cursor decoders now decrypt directly into
  zeroizing buffers, rather than receiving a plain plaintext `Vec` on AEAD
  success. The decoded token bytes also scrub on drop; a failed authentication
  drops its in-place buffer under the same guard. Red-first synthetic tests
  for both codecs prove guarded return types, valid decryption, and tamper
  refusal; browser/application memory remains outside this custody boundary.

- All ten CDP `Network.getResponseBody` commands now route through a single
  helper holding temporary request-ID JSON in the strict scrub-on-drop guard,
  not an ordinary `json!` temporary. The helper keeps the exact Network
  allowlist and passive event observation path unchanged, while the returned
  response still uses guarded JSON decoding.

- Passive CDP Network request-ID map keys and completed-ID set entries now
  have a shared bounded scrub-on-remove, scrub-on-retain, and scrub-on-drop
  custody helper. It preserves exact lookup and lifecycle membership while
  erasing retired correlation identifiers from local heap allocations. Red-first
  type assertions and synthetic key-membership tests cover the new boundary;
  caller-owned response tuples and browser buffers remain outside this guard.

- In-flight message-list continuation `AnchorID` selectors are now held in
  zeroizing custody through exact URL/response matching. Completed requests
  retain the explicit caller-facing identifier tuple, and tracker removal
  erases the extra stored selector copy. Existing bounded batch fixtures
  continue to prove continuation identity requirements.

- Exact message, conversation, and attachment Network request trackers now
  guard provider entity selectors in `Zeroizing<String>`. Request retries and
  failed or consumed lifecycle entries erase their stored selector strings;
  callers still receive the same bounded explicit identifier fields. No
  request URL, headers, or body are retained by these trackers.

- Finished message-list request state now erases private request IDs and
  cursor-anchor identifiers when dropped. The captured list bundle scrubs
  its sort label and moves the owned response vector with `take` during
  single-batch extraction; red-first Drop checks and existing batch/paging
  fixtures retain previous behavior.

- All passive read-only Network collectors now retain their flattened CDP
  session identifier in `Zeroizing<String>`, including request-list captures,
  exact message/conversation/attachment captures, and catalog observation.
  This erases session identity copies on success, rejection, and early drop;
  synthetic lifecycle tests continue to enforce exact-session correlation.

- The strict provider/browser JSON decoder no longer exposes ordinary owned
  response trees in production: both plaintext-text and byte-slice test-only
  decoders are isolated behind `cfg(test)`. Production receives only a
  scrub-on-drop `SensitiveValue`, including partial parse failures. Outbound
  CDP commands now serialize straight into zeroizing frame bytes, covering
  partial writer failures rather than guarding only successful serialization.

- Opaque encrypted catalog continuation tokens and per-generation snapshot
  boundary IDs now scrub on drop. Nested catalog items already erase private
  names and identifiers, and the serialized page maintains its public cursor
  and entry accessors. Red-first type checks and pagination/resume fixtures
  retain the existing authenticated, scope-bound cursor behavior.

- Catalog labels/folders and their parents, list row identifiers, exact
  conversation member and parent IDs, and attachment metadata identifiers
  now erase retained strings on drop. Nested item vectors retain explicit
  element-level custody, while projection into browser-independent typed
  responses and redacted Debug behavior remain unchanged. Compile-failing
  first-pass tests prove the previously missing Drop contracts, and synthetic
  parser tests preserve message and conversation membership behavior.

- Retained single-message metadata now erases copied subject, sender/recipient
  names and addresses, label and identity strings, and original attachment
  names on drop. The reusable sanitized filename leaf also scrubs its String
  allocation, including when held inside a message descriptor or attachment
  output policy. Existing metadata accessors and synthetic compatibility tests
  remain unchanged; red-first Drop checks establish explicit custody. This
  covers successfully retained objects, not temporary parsing copies or
  browser-owned content buffers.

- Full decoded provider response JSON now stays in a `SensitiveValue` guard
  through every bounded read projector, including metadata-only message,
  conversation, attachment, catalog, and event readers. The guard erases
  unselected body/header/cryptographic fields and map keys on return or
  projection failure; only explicitly projected metadata survives. Synthetic
  ownership and malformed JSON regressions preserve strict parsing. This
  protects local JSON allocations, not browser/OS copies or retained provider
  metadata.

- Mailbox/label catalog metadata can be captured passively and retained as
  immutable in-memory snapshots, then paged with authenticated
  account/generation/query-bound cursors without rereading Proton during
  continuation. Public transport DTO fields remain undefined by the frozen
  contract, so no wire shape is invented.
- Provider literal `Address` fields now reject U+FFFD replacement glyphs:
  a possibly lossily decoded sender/recipient cannot establish an exact
  future reply target. Red-first message-detail tests cover Sender, To and
  Cc while ordinary subjects retain this visible character.

- Provider literal sender/recipient `Address` values now reject embedded
  address-list or display-wrapper delimiters (comma, semicolon, colon and
  angle brackets). One field must not silently carry several reply targets;
  red-first detail-response tests retain punctuated display names and
  plus-tagged ordinary addresses.

- Literal sender and recipient mailbox addresses now reject embedded
  ASCII and Unicode whitespace, not merely leading/trailing space. A future
  reply target cannot silently treat a malformed multi-token address as one
  provider identity; red-first message-detail tests preserve ordinary display
  names and plus-tagged addresses.

- Display metadata at both read boundaries now rejects additional hidden
  Unicode formatting/filler markers already refused by route-ID validation.
  Browser-side visible rows refuse them before CDP serialization; Rust visible
  rows and message-detail subjects, display names, and literal addresses also
  fail closed. Red-first synthetic JavaScript and projection tests cover the
  formerly accepted markers while preserving ordinary printable metadata.

- Exact single-message `mail/v4/messages/{messageID}` responses can now be
  reduced to bounded identity/envelope/read-state/label/MIME and attachment
  descriptor metadata while dropping body, headers, passwords, cryptographic
  packets, and attachment bytes. The managed browser can passively wait for the
  next app-owned detail GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or
  injecting
  a provider call, with clean timeout and ambiguity handling. This still does
  not satisfy targeted `get_message`: current WebClients may use cached message
  body without a network fetch, and plaintext body/header plus attachment
  decryption evidence remains open.

- Provider-neutral attachment output now has pure safety primitives before any
  filesystem access. Untrusted provider filenames are reduced to one portable
  UTF-8 leaf capped at 255 bytes, path separators/control characters are
  neutralized, leading-dot and Windows device-name hazards are removed, and
  diagnostics redact the resulting filename.
- Caller attachment destinations normalize only when strictly relative and
  reject parent traversal, absolute roots, backslashes, control characters, and
  drive/alternate-stream colon syntax. Inline eligibility uses the frozen
  `32 KiB` contract ceiling exactly.
- The attachment output root rejects a group/world-writable or foreign-owned
  final root at open time. A further privacy gate requires owner-only rwx
  directories (0700-equivalent) at the root and each nested destination parent:
  otherwise local users could list sensitive attachment filenames even though
  the attachment contents are stored in 0600 files. Synthetic tests cover
  readable and writable permission refusal without changing caller modes.
- The local runtime now owns bounded file output under an explicit absolute
  root. It opens every root/destination directory component through held file
  descriptors with no-follow semantics, enforces the configured byte ceiling
  before creating output, writes a private `0600` `.partial` inode, and commits
  with an atomic hard link that never overwrites an existing entry. Synthetic
  tests cover nested output, root and destination symlinks, later root-path
  replacement, existing file/symlink refusal, missing parents, oversized bytes,
  private mode, single-link cleanup, and redacted diagnostics. This boundary is
  not yet wired to a public attachment tool because plaintext browser retrieval
  remains unresolved.

- Exact attachment metadata responses now have a separate bounded projector for
  `GET /api/mail/v4/attachments/{attachment_id}/metadata`. It requires provider
  success, binds body `Attachment.ID` to the requested attachment, and binds
  `Attachment.MessageID` to the caller-expected parent message before retaining
  only sanitized filename, declared size, and MIME type. A target-session CDP
  classifier tracks only exact metadata GET lifecycles, accepts only HTTP-200
  JSON with stable attachment identity, discards failed app retries, and caps
  unfinished matching requests at 16. Sender/address data, conversation
  identity, key packets, signatures, request headers, and cookies are discarded.
- The shared MIME parser for attachment and message detail no longer
  allocates ordinary lowercase copies of parameter names from scrub-on-drop
  provider JSON. It checks case-insensitive duplicates against borrowed input
  slices, preserving the existing 512-byte field limit, quoted values, and
  nonadjacent duplicate refusal in synthetic tests.
- The managed browser can now wait passively for the next app-owned attachment
  metadata GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or injecting a provider
  request. It bounds the wait to 30 seconds, enables Network with a 256 KiB
  resource and 1 MiB total buffer, projects only the completed exact metadata
  response, and always disables Network before returning success, timeout, or
  projection failure. A response must still bind to the caller-expected parent
  message, concurrent/residual matching requests fail as ambiguous, and a clean
  timeout is `None`. Synthetic browser tests cover success, no-action timeout,
  parent mismatch cleanup, and a request starting during Network teardown.
- Current WebClients fetches encrypted attachment bytes and full metadata in
  parallel, derives the attachment session key, then calls `decryptAndVerify`
  client-side. Therefore the metadata projector proves message-scoped identity
  and safe metadata only; it does not make encrypted HTTP bytes equivalent to
  the plaintext `attachment_v1` response.

- Exact unparameterized conversation responses now have a separate bounded
  membership projector and target-session Network classifier for
  `GET /api/mail/v4/conversations/{conversation_id}`. It requires provider
  success, exact response/request conversation identity, a bounded
  `Conversation.NumMessages`, `Messages.length == NumMessages`, unique message
  IDs, and every member `ConversationID` matching the parent. Only member ID and
  numeric `Time` survive, in provider response order; bodies, headers, subject,
  recipients, attachments, and cryptographic fields are discarded.
- The classifier rejects `?MessageID=...` conversation requests because their
  slicing/completeness semantics are not yet proven. This establishes exact
  provider thread membership for one complete response, not public
  `get_thread`: plaintext members still require independently bounded reads and
  the provider-neutral ordered thread mapping remains unwired.
- The managed browser can now wait passively for the next complete app-owned
  unparameterized conversation GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or
  injecting a provider request. It bounds the wait to 30 seconds, uses bounded
  Network buffers, projects only the completed exact response, and disables
  Network before success, clean timeout, or projection failure. Residual or late
  exact conversation requests fail as ambiguous. Synthetic browser tests cover
  complete success, no-action timeout, incomplete membership cleanup, and a
  request starting during Network teardown.
- Conversation list/search responses now have a separate bounded projector and
  exact-endpoint Network classifier. Fresh responses require `Stale=0`, no
  active `TasksRunning`, bounded `Total`, at most 100 unique conversations, and
  numeric `Time`, provider `Order`, and `NumMessages`; only those fields plus
  opaque conversation ID survive. Request/response URL identity is bound with
  randomized in-memory fingerprints, so search terms and query text are not
  retained. This intentionally does not interpret Page/Anchor continuation or
  claim snapshot/order compatibility for public `list_threads`/search; those
  provider-neutral semantics remain separate work.
- The conversation-list response projector can now match stable visible row
  IDs to one already-observed response, returning only ID, numeric Time,
  provider Order, and message count in rendered order. Missing/duplicated
  visible IDs, excessive rows, and contradictory empty-page evidence fail
  closed. It does not by itself identify conversation render mode, tie a
  request to an immutable page, or permit public list/search operations.
- The managed browser now offers an explicitly passive, bounded wait for the
  next app-owned exact conversation-list GET. It checks ready Mail shell and
  origin before/after the capture, disables Network on success, timeout, or
  projection failure, and rejects residual concurrent requests. Synthetic
  Chromium-pipe tests cover successful fresh projection, clean timeout, stale
  response cleanup, and a request starting during Network teardown. No
  provider-neutral thread search, pagination, or immutable snapshot claim is
  implied.

- A new managed-browser diagnostic couples those bounded list metadata rows
  with a same-session content-free before/after visible list. It blocks known
  message-only routes, loading, changed row IDs or pagination, and absent
  provider IDs. It never navigates or fetches provider data itself. This does
  not bind a Mail route to a cursor, prove conversation mode when unknown, or
  implement public `list_threads`.

- Conversation-list GET captures now retain sanitized request page and anchor
  presence, never full query text. The visible-ID diagnostic rejects missing
  or inconsistent request page numbers and anchor continuations, and does not
  treat a bare ID match as sufficient page evidence. This is deliberately
  narrower than provider-neutral paginated list/search and carries no server
  snapshot consistency guarantee.

- Explicitly empty visible conversation evidence now requires both an empty
  provider row set and `Total=0`. A syntactically fresh response with zero
  returned rows but positive provider total is treated as contradictory,
  without inferring that a later page represents an empty mailbox.

- For visible-ID diagnostics, sanitized conversation-list page/anchor
  evidence is now checked immediately after the app-owned GET lifecycle and
  *before* requesting its response body. Requests for another page or an
  anchor continuation fail without reading provider JSON at all; Network is
  still disabled before surfacing the error. The independent, explicitly
  passive raw-list observer retains its previous behavior.

- The passive conversation-list classifier now rejects contradictory numeric
  paging metadata (`PageSize != Limit`) and a numbered `Page` mixed with
  `Anchor`/`AnchorID`. These are separate initial-versus-continuation shapes
  in the reviewed WebClients request path; rejecting their mixture prevents
  a false positive page match without interpreting any search/label text.

- A consumed conversation-list CDP request ID remains terminal until the
  observation ends. Bounded content-free completion markers prevent a late
  replay/failure after the original request has been handed off from silently
  bypassing lifecycle checks. This does not establish immutable result paging
  or enable public thread search.

- The same bounded terminal-ID protection now covers complete conversation,
  single-message detail, and attachment-metadata GET captures. Consumption
  retains only the CDP request ID (not content), so late lifecycle events
  cannot be mistaken for unrelated background traffic. Synthetic fake-browser
  replay tests exercise each diagnostic without new provider action authority.

- The visible-message batch Network classifier now keeps up to 128 consumed
  request IDs as terminal, preserving request-context checks while detecting
  late replies or failures during teardown. A synthetic reload/disable replay
  proves subsequent safe browser inspection still works. This strengthens
  diagnostics without changing public read/search availability.

- CDP request-ID retention is now bounded to 512 bytes across message-list,
  message-detail, conversation-detail, attachment-metadata, and conversation-
  list reads. This applies only to exact app-owned GET shapes, not other
  background traffic; empty/oversized matching IDs fail closed without being
  persisted into capture state or debug diagnostics.

- Visible conversation metadata now requires both PageSize and Limit to
  agree alongside a numeric initial Page, not merely a matching page number.
  Missing initial-request shape fails before `Network.getResponseBody` and
  does not affect the independent passive diagnostic; this still does not
  create snapshot isolation or public thread-list support.

- Visible conversation reconciliation additionally refuses `BeginID`,
  `EndID`, and `ID` selectors, because these need not describe the currently
  rendered page even when Page/PageSize/Limit agree. The presence-only flag is
  kept internally; selector values and private query text are discarded.

- Mailbox/label catalog capture now retains consumed CDP request IDs until
  observation teardown. A bounded 16-request ledger and 512-byte ID ceiling
  prevent late lifecycle replays and unbounded diagnostics; unrelated contact
  groups remain ignored. Synthetic browser tests verify failure recovery and
  safe subsequent Mail-shell inspection.

- Network lifecycle observers now reject late loading failures on already
  finished but unconsumed catalog, message-detail, conversation-detail,
  attachment-metadata, and event GETs. This closes the gap between receipt and
  consumption of a safe response; actual pre-completion app failures continue
  to permit app-owned retries without additional adapter provider requests.

- Exact detail GET identities now refuse backslash characters as opaque URL
  path separators for messages, conversations, and attachment metadata.
  Synthetic CDP requests prove those ambiguous paths fail closed before any
  response body can be read or mapped to provider-neutral identifiers.

- Message-list CDP request URLs are now bounded to 16 KiB before hashing
  untrusted query context, with the same bound enforced on matching responses.
  Message and conversation list URL classifiers also refuse fragments as
  non-network identities. This prevents synthetic fragment/query drift from
  being mistaken for app-owned list evidence without inventing public paging.

- The bounded message-list query fingerprint now preserves the relative
  order of repeated keys while canonicalizing distinct query-key placement.
  This is a conservative identity check rather than provider search semantics;
  unknown repeated filter values cannot reorder silently between batches.

- Direct list response projection now enforces the capture's 16 KiB URL
  input ceiling for both messages and conversations; even standalone parser
  calls cannot interpret an overlong exact list endpoint as safe metadata.

- Message-list and conversation-list observation now agree with the other
  read captures: `loadingFailed` after `loadingFinished` is a lifecycle
  contradiction, even before the completed request has been consumed. Pure
  synthetic tests prove the completed request remains recoverable for cleanup.

- Projected message and conversation list IDs now use the same bounded
  route-component safety rules as their detail-read endpoints, additionally
  disallowing Unicode whitespace and controls. Rendered-row reconciliation
  fails before metadata lookup on such IDs; provider content never enters
  an error or debug diagnostic. This is still not public paginated reading.

- Complete conversation membership now rejects unsafe member-message and
  parent identifiers as well as unsafe exact detail URL components, aligning
  its projected IDs with bounded list-read route safety. Only synthetic
  identity metadata is retained; plaintext thread reading remains blocked.

- Message-detail projection now rejects malformed exact message URL IDs,
  unsafe parent conversation identifiers, label IDs, and attachment descriptor
  IDs, while preserving content-bearing metadata and valid printable IDs.
  This remains bounded metadata only; body decryption and headers are not read.

- Attachment metadata projection now validates both attachment and expected
  parent-message IDs as bounded, safe opaque route components before reading
  bodies. Invalid caller identities or provider body fields fail closed;
  filename and MIME processing remain independent of identity safety.

- System folder, custom folder, and label catalog IDs now enforce safe
  opaque identifier rules independently of their user-defined display names.
  Nested `ParentID` values receive the same checks; safe printable IDs and
  non-route display text continue to project without changing public tools.

- Message-list continuation anchors are now checked as bounded safe opaque
  IDs rather than length-only strings; malformed separators and Unicode
  whitespace fail before a continuation can become list evidence.

- Visible mailbox-list inspection now enforces safe opaque row IDs before
  content-bearing page snapshots can inherit them. Synthetic browser tests
  reject whitespace and separator-bearing DOM attributes in the earliest
  content-free list read, rather than relying on later network reconciliation.

- Every route-bound read identifier now rejects the special path segments
  `.` and `..`, in addition to whitespace, controls, and delimiters. Synthetic
  list, detail, catalog and visible-page evidence closes path normalization
  ambiguity before provider-neutral read or cursor processing.

- The read-only adapter now centralizes route-bound opaque-ID validation in
  a private, content-free module shared by list, message/conversation detail,
  attachment metadata, catalog, event, and rendered-page evidence. This is
  an internal defensive boundary, not a newly advertised public read feature.

- Percent-escaped path components and selected invisible Unicode format
  controls now fail the shared read-ID predicate before list, detail,
  attachment, catalog and visible-row identity can be exposed. The validator
  never decodes or normalizes provider IDs; user-visible names and content
  fields retain their independent bounds.

- Exact conversation-list response URLs are bounded at 16 KiB before any
  private request-correlation hash is computed, matching the request limit.
  Synthetic boundary checks prove the largest accepted URL and fail closed
  on an oversized response without producing list metadata.

- Conversation-list continuation parsing now refuses malformed or
  overlong `AnchorID` values and non-decimal/overflowing numeric `Anchor`
  timestamps. This prevents unsafe request-shape evidence from reaching a
  visible-page diagnostic, while leaving full cursor/snapshot proof open.

- Message-list numeric pagination selectors now require canonical bounded
  ASCII-decimal values, removing alternate encodings of page, size, direction,
  and time anchors before any visible-list request can be correlated.

- Conversation-list page and size selectors now use the same decimal-only
  shape policy as the message list, refusing ambiguous signed or encoded
  numbers before a browser page may be associated with a Network response.

- Exact label/catalog `Type` selectors now reject alternate numeric
  representations, so a decoded catalog cannot be attributed to a loosely
  parsed category. Unsupported canonical numeric types remain unobserved.

- Numeric list page and continuation shape validators now reject
  leading-zero aliases, preventing noncanonical URLs from matching the same
  rendered page or continuation timestamp during passive request capture.

- Content-free visible pagination indicators now use the same canonical
  numeric page representation as Network list requests, so a noncanonical
  DOM page marker cannot be reconciled with a different app-owned GET.

- The full custom-folder catalog projector now verifies that references
  among captured folders do not form cycles. It preserves external/root
  parent identifiers without assuming that all parent IDs name catalog rows.

- Visible pagination now rejects an enabled successor from page `u32::MAX`
  before the read-only adapter can attempt impossible numeric page advancement.

- Passive message-list metadata now honors an explicitly unsuccessful
  provider `Code` rather than accepting fresh-looking rows from it. Missing
  optional `Code` remains compatible pending verified wire-schema evidence;
  browser tests cover the typed rejection without exposing message content.

- Explicitly empty visible message pages now reject a positive *initial*
  provider total even if the response row array is empty. The initial Total
  is authoritative; anchored continuation totals may legitimately describe
  a different result scope and are not compared to the initial batch.
  This remains diagnostic and does not establish immutable pagination.

- Header research found two distinct fields on the exact message-detail
  response. `Message.Header` is raw header text, while `Message.ParsedHeaders`
  maps names to either one string or an array of strings; WebClients reads the
  parsed form for message semantics and combines raw `Header` with
  MIME-generated
  headers when exporting a message. The frozen contract has no standalone
  `get_headers` capability and only says `message_v1` contains headers requested
  by the caller. Its transport DTO/header-selection and normalization shape is
  still undefined, so the adapter continues to discard both fields rather than
  inventing casing, duplicate-value ordering, or raw-versus-parsed semantics.

- Rendered-body research confirms the opened message article exposes the
  provider
  ID through `data-message-id` and its same-origin content iframe exposes
  `#proton-root`. That is not yet a complete message-body source: WebClients can
  remove collapsed quoted content from the iframe DOM, applies renderer/search
  transforms, and marks unread messages read after expansion/body load. A future
  body projection must remain passive and prove completeness before mapping this
  surface to provider-neutral message body semantics.

- Exact message-detail projection now rejects C0/C1 controls and Unicode
  line/paragraph separators in subjects and recipient display fields, agreeing
  with the visible-row boundary before later public read or reply mapping.
  Synthetic red-first fixtures preserve ordinary Unicode metadata.

- A complete fake-browser message-detail regression now injects a Unicode
  line separator into the provider subject and verifies projection refusal,
  Network cleanup, and continued same-session CDP readiness without exposing
  the malformed detail metadata.

- Non-ASCII Unicode spacing characters (including nonbreaking, figure,
  narrow and ideographic spaces) now fail the portable destination
  boundary and are replaced with `_` in provider-supplied filenames.
  Red-first tests also retain ordinary ASCII spaces in filenames.

- Mongolian Free Variation Selector Four (U+180F) now receives the same
  neutralization as the other invisible filename modifiers; provider
  filenames replace it with `_`, while caller-supplied output paths
  reject it. Synthetic red-first tests establish both boundaries.

- Provider-neutral attachment filename sanitization now replaces Unicode
  line separators, bidirectional and invisible formatting controls, and
  blank-looking filler characters with underscores. Red-first pure-domain
  regressions cover extension-spoofing names while preserving real Unicode
  letters in otherwise safe filenames.

- Both single-message and standalone attachment-metadata projection paths
  now have synthetic regressions proving that bidi-spoofed provider filenames
  retain only a neutralized saved-name leaf, while Debug remains redacted.

- Caller-supplied attachment output destinations now reject the same invisible
  Unicode and direction controls as provider filenames, rather than allowing
  a misleading path leaf to cross the portable relative-path boundary.
  Synthetic tests prove rejection and preserve ordinary Unicode directories.

- Windows console device leaves `CONIN$` and `CONOUT$` are now handled
  alongside classical DOS devices for both provider-sanitized filenames and
  caller-relative destinations. Synthetic red-first regressions cover case
  folding and extension aliases, informed by Microsoft Console CreateFile
  documentation without copying implementation from upstream.

- Relative attachment destinations now refuse trailing and doubled slash
  spellings that Rust would otherwise collapse into a different file leaf.
  Red-first synthetic tests cover these directory/file aliases while keeping
  explicit current-directory (`./`) component normalization intact.

- Caller attachment paths now reject trailing current-directory selectors
  (`exports/file/.`) before normalization. They previously collapsed into a
  different file leaf, despite looking like directory references. Red-first
  synthetic cases preserve accepted interior `./` path components.

- Caller-supplied output paths now refuse Windows-forbidden punctuation
  (`?`, `*`, quotes, angle brackets, `|`) in every component, aligning their
  portable syntax with provider filename sanitization. Synthetic regression
  evidence proved each was previously accepted.

- Caller-supplied attachment relative output paths now have enforced 4,096-byte
  total size, 255-byte per-component size, and 64-component depth limits before
  any filesystem work. A red-first synthetic regression covers ASCII and UTF-8
  component overflow, deep paths, cumulative overlong paths, and valid limits.

- AttachmentRelativePath rejects Windows reserved device components and
  trailing-dot/trailing-space components before any local I/O, preventing
  cross-platform output aliases despite Unix accepting those names. Red-first
  synthetic tests exercise nested folders and final leaves while preserving
  ordinary Unicode and safe similarly named files.
  The portable-device check also handles Windows-recognized superscript
  one/two/three COM/LPT aliases in both provider filename sanitization and
  caller paths; both regressions were demonstrated failing before the fix.
  Device naming follows Microsoft's published Windows filename restrictions;
  no platform-specific filesystem probing is needed at runtime.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every path is classified read-only by the tool contract.
- Search, pagination, Unicode, MIME, and malformed-message cases are tested.
- Attachment traversal and oversized outputs fail safely.
- Logs contain identifiers and timing only, never content.
- The primary web UI adapter passes every read workflow; no paid Bridge or
  direct-API client identity is an unstated prerequisite.

## Evidence

- Synthetic mailbox fixtures
- Contract and adapter integration tests

- Attachment filename sanitization and caller-chosen relative destination paths
  now reject Khmer inherent vowel signs (U+17B4–U+17B5) and shorthand
  formatting controls (U+1BCA0–U+1BCA3). Red-first tests confirmed that
  these default-ignorable marks previously survived filename normalization
  or were accepted as output-path components. Ordinary Unicode stays intact.

- Attachment filename sanitization and caller-chosen output destination paths
  now reject the additional Unicode 16 formatting controls also refused by
  the read-only provider-ID boundary. A red-first synthetic regression proved
  that Arabic, Syriac, annotation, Kaithi, hieroglyph, and musical markers
  previously survived as filename/path text; printable Unicode stays valid.

- Filesystem-facing attachment names now reject lossy Unicode replacement
  U+FFFD: provider filename leaves map it to `_`, while a caller-provided
  destination with that glyph fails closed before filesystem I/O. The
  red-first regression exposed the previously accepted alias and preserves
  genuine accented/CJK filename characters. Free-form message subjects are
  unaffected.

- Attachment filename sanitization now replaces Unicode noncharacters with
  `_`, and caller-supplied relative output paths reject them before filesystem
  access. Red-first pure fixtures covered U+FDD0/U+FDEF, BMP U+FFFE, and
  supplementary plane endings through U+10FFFF; normal Unicode filenames
  remain portable and unmodified.

- The file-output partial inode now uses fresh OS-generated 128-bit random
  names rather than predictable process IDs and sequence numbers. A red-first
  synthetic directory fixture pre-created 512 old-style guesses and proved
  that those guesses could previously exhaust the bounded create attempts;
  fresh output now succeeds without modifying any existing attacker files.

- Final attachment output parents now refuse group- or world-writable
  directory permissions before any temporary file creation. Red-first
  synthetic tests covered both output roots and nested parents with 0770
  and 0777 modes; each now fails closed without committing bytes. This
  limits cross-account replacement of an observed temporary name, while
  same-user concurrent mutation still requires cautious filesystem use.

- Nested attachment output now validates directory permissions at every held
  component from the configured root through the final parent, not just the
  leaf parent. A red-first synthetic regression covered both 0770 and 0777
  permissions on the root and an intermediate directory above an otherwise
  private final parent; these now fail before any partial file is created.

- Reserved Windows-device attachment filenames are rechecked for trailing
  dots/spaces after prefix insertion and UTF-8 byte-limit truncation. Without
  the second trim, an originally internal character could become a terminal
  nonportable alias at the 255-byte boundary. Red-first synthetic cases for
  `CON`, `LPT1`, and `NUL` prove the previously exposed suffix is removed.

- Explicit attachment output roots now validate each held absolute-path
  ancestor before descending into the requested root. A private 0700 output
  root beneath a non-sticky shared-writable parent could previously be
  renamed by another local account even though nested output checks passed.
  A red-first regression proves 0770/0777 parents are rejected before output;
  trusted sticky directories, such as `/tmp`, remain compatible.

- Each held attachment output root and nested directory now requires the
  effective process UID as owner, in addition to private mode. A foreign-owned
  0700 directory was previously considered safe by mode alone, although its
  owner can change permissions or move its entries. A red-first pure policy
  regression proves foreign owner rejection, including root-owned output when
  the process is not root; normal owned private directories still pass.

- Visible mailbox-row subject/address text now refuses visually hidden
  formatting and bidi override/isolate code points at both the browser
  JavaScript projection and the Rust CDP-response validation. Exact message
  detail Subject and sender/recipient Name/Address use the same fail-closed
  policy. Red-first pure-Rust and synthetic Node DOM tests demonstrate the
  old acceptance and verify rejection without leaking private metadata.
  Ordinary printable Unicode remains readable; this still does not enable
  plaintext body/header extraction or public get-message transport.
- Refined the display-text policy with red-first positive Rust/Node tests:
  emoji variation selectors and script joiners are valid in message subjects
  and display names. Bidirectional overrides, hidden separators and line
  controls remain refused, while literal message addresses retain a stricter
  safety rule. Opaque route IDs remain unchanged and strictly validated.

## Nested attachment parent binding

- A red-first synthetic detail-response fixture showed that a provider
  `Attachment.MessageID` could disagree with the surrounding, URL-bound
  `Message.ID` and still be retained as an addressable attachment. The
  metadata-only projector now rejects a contradictory parent as a typed
  `AttachmentParentMismatch` and malformed asserted IDs as `Malformed`. An
  absent or null optional parent remains compatible with existing provider
  shapes; a matching asserted parent is accepted.
- A fake Chromium passive-capture fixture confirms that a contradictory
  nested parent fails with a typed error, disables Network and retains safe
  same-session Mail-shell inspection. Independent attachment metadata GETs
  still require the caller-bound parent before output; this does not enable
  plaintext attachment retrieval or public read tools.

## Duplicate-key ambiguity in read JSON

- Untrusted provider response and CDP JSON previously used the last value for
  duplicate object keys. This could mask conflicting provider `Code`, message
  IDs or nested attachment metadata before policy checks. A shared strict
  Serde visitor now rejects duplicates recursively, including equivalent
  escaped key names, without changing valid JSON projections.
- All bounded web-adapter provider response projectors, browser CDP frame
  readers and authenticated in-memory cursor payload decoders now use it.
  Red-first synthetic detail tests, parser parity cases and fake-browser
  cleanup evidence cover ambiguity rejection without provider contact.

- The catalog, complete-conversation, and single-message attachment
  projectors now use explicitly borrowed ID sets backed by the guarded
  provider JSON. Duplicate validation does not create unzeroized temporary
  String clones; returned metadata keeps its existing scrub-on-drop custody.
  Synthetic nonadjacent duplicate and distinct-prefix tests preserve exact
  rejection and ordering without fetching any provider data.

- The managed CDP driver now guards temporary completed network-request
  batches before selecting a single response; ambiguous replies scrub all
  copied request IDs, optional anchor IDs, and other metadata on failure;
  a single accepted row transfers into its existing scrub-on-drop model.
  Catalog batches also wrap each copied request ID before parsing or rejecting
  duplicate kinds. ID-only accessors scrub discarded event and anchor IDs.
  Synthetic probes verify every rejected row is erased, while empty/single
  results retain their previous behavior.

- Successful passive CDP request selection now places the copied request
  selector (and any conversation, message, attachment, or event ID companion)
  into zeroizing custody before cancellation, page validation, body fetch, or
  parse can fail. This extends rejected-batch protection to accepted rows;
  a synthetic abort-path probe verifies both single and paired drop scrubbing.

## CDP command-reply stream ambiguity

- A red-first fake-browser regression found the central CDP command-response
  reader still used last-wins JSON even after passive readers were hardened.
  Its parser now rejects duplicated unsolicited method keys, remembers the
  protocol error, drains the authentic command reply and disables Network.
  Subsequent same-session shell inspection remains possible. This closes the
  remaining web-adapter raw `Value` JSON decoding exception.

- The recursive strict JSON visitor now scrubs partial decoded strings and
  object keys on parser failure, including duplicate nested keys and malformed
  nested arrays. Previously an error inside a partly populated map/array
  destroyed plain `Value` strings before the authenticated cursor's outer
  `SensitiveValue` could own them. Intermediate `StrictValue` containers now
  implement `Drop` and transfer completed children through `take`; a red-first
  compile-time ownership test and malformed/duplicate synthetic inputs cover
  this early-failure path. Provider JSON values returned successfully to
  non-cursor read projectors retain their existing ownership semantics.

- CDP frame decoding now applies the existing `SensitiveValue` JSON guard to
  every inbound browser notification and reply in the read-only driver.
  `Network.requestWillBeSent` can carry Cookie/Authorization headers before
  a narrow observer discards the event; previously the strict parser returned
  an ordinary `Value`, leaving those raw decoded strings unzeroized on drop.
  One private decoding boundary covers all 11 inbound call sites, including
  invalid or irrelevant events. Red-first typed tests prove the decode guard
  and strict duplicate refusal; normal returned command result values retain
  their existing caller-owned representation.

- A follow-up to strict JSON partial-tree scrubbing now guards object keys
  before decoding their values. Serde's combined `next_entry<String, ...>`
  previously released an already-decoded key as an ordinary String if the
  associated value failed to parse. The visitor now uses separate `next_key`
  and `next_value` calls with `Zeroizing<String>` intermediate ownership;
  completed keys move into the existing scrub-on-drop object guard without
  creating copies. A red-first typed key test and malformed nested-map cases
  preserve strict refusal and ordinary successful projection.

- Read-only CDP replies and runtime projections now keep scrub-on-drop JSON
  custody after separating the validated command `result` from its envelope.
  Previously `result.clone()` returned ordinary `Value`, and a later
  `Runtime.evaluate` by-value extraction cloned another unguarded tree. The
  private call chain now returns `SensitiveValue`, moving nested objects with
  `take_object_field()` and preserving strict response validation. Synthetic
  type-contract and nested value tests guard the return paths without a live
  browser; caller-owned normalized mail outputs retain their existing public
  representation and must be handled as sensitive application data.

- Passive message-list and conversation-list Network captures now reject
  CDP responses explicitly sourced from disk cache, prefetch cache, or service
  workers, including malformed source-marker types. A provider body advertising
  `Stale: 0` does not override contradictory transport provenance. Red-first
  synthetic CDP events prove both formerly accepted cached list replies fail
  closed; omitted or explicit-false source flags remain compatible.

- Passive system-folder, custom-folder and label catalog Network responses now
  also reject explicit disk, service-worker and prefetch-cache provenance, plus
  malformed source flags. Red-first synthetic responses previously authorized
  cached catalog metadata; ordinary GETs with omitted or false flags still
  complete. This does not independently prove provider response freshness.

- Passive exact message-detail, conversation-detail and attachment-metadata
  response captures now reject cached, prefetched or service-worker-sourced
  CDP evidence, including malformed provenance flags. Red-first synthetic
  requests proved these three endpoints previously accepted cached metadata;
  normal exact GETs with omitted or explicitly false flags remain valid.
  This is transport-source checking, not proof of server freshness.

- Exact detail and attachment observers now also reject CDP's standalone
  `Network.requestServedFromCache` event for the matching request ID, whether
  it arrives before or after responseReceived. This covers memory-cache
  provenance that cannot be excluded by response source flags alone. Red-first
  synthetic events prove the prior acceptance; unrelated cache traffic stays
  ignored and no extra provider data is retained.

- The message-list, conversation-list and mailbox catalog passive Network
  captures now reject standalone CDP `Network.requestServedFromCache` events
  for matching GET request IDs before or after responseReceived. Red-first
  synthetic lifecycle tests exercise both orders, all catalog types and
  unrelated cache traffic; response flags alone cannot exclude memory cache.

- The passive list, catalog, detail and attachment captures now reject the
  additional Chromium `fromEarlyHints` response flag, which CDP specifies as
  prefetch-cache provenance. Explicit true or malformed values fail closed;
  omitted and false values remain compatible. Red-first tests reproduced this
  gap independently across every observed provider GET type.

- All passive read response observers now share one private cache-provenance
  validator, eliminating seven formerly duplicated predicates. In addition to
  the four source flags, the shared gate rejects any reported service-worker
  response source, Cache Storage name or service-worker router metadata, even
  when earlier flags conflict. Red-first synthetic evidence covers each exact
  GET family; omitted hints and explicit-false source flags preserve reads.

- Passive message, conversation, attachment, list and catalog observers now
  inspect `Network.responseReceivedExtraInfo.statusCode` when present. CDP may
  report HTTP 200 in `responseReceived` but an actual cache revalidation 304
  here, before or after that event. Tracked requests reject all non-200 or
  malformed extra statuses; unrelated requests are ignored. The shared helper
  checks status only without retaining headers or response body.

  Red-first
  synthetic 304 events reproduced the acceptance gap.

- A synthetic end-to-end CDP pipe now verifies that separate cache-hit and
  304 network-status notifications propagate through `ManagedBrowser`, reject
  exact message detail evidence before body retrieval, and still invoke
  `Network.disable`. The browser session remains usable for subsequent Mail
  shell checks. No live provider account or message data is needed.

- All passive read Network observers now refuse late `responseReceivedExtraInfo`
  HTTP-200 notices after the exact request ID was consumed. Such late events
  no longer bypass the existing completed-ID replay rule; tests first proved
  acceptance in message, conversation, attachment, list and catalog flows.

- Synthetic full-browser CDP tests now inject late HTTP 200 and 304 extra-info
  notices for a consumed message-detail request while `Network.disable` is
  pending. Both produce invalid-sequence failures before returning projected
  private metadata, despite response body access having occurred earlier; the
  CDP session remains usable after teardown.

- CDP can declare `hasExtraInfo: true` on `Network.responseReceived` while
  deferring the actual HTTP status to `responseReceivedExtraInfo`. Previously,
  read captures consumed `loadingFinished` without waiting for that promised
  status, potentially overlooking a subsequent cache-revalidation 304. The
  shared state gate now retains completed requests until the exact HTTP-200
  extra status arrives, even after `loadingFinished`; malformed declarations,
  duplicate notices and contradictory false declarations fail closed.

  Red-first
  tests cover all passive GET families. A fake Chromium pipe also proves that
  absent status blocks message-body access and delayed 200 permits it without
  leaking message content or leaving Network enabled.

- Pending extra-status promises retain completed message-detail GETs until
  their actual status arrives, but cannot bypass the 16-request lifecycle
  budget. Synthetic saturation tests prove each unconfirmed response remains
  unconsumed and the next request is rejected without exposing request IDs.
